Contributing
This catalog is only useful if it stays current. AI-specific threats change faster than most security taxonomies, so contributions that keep it aligned with maintained external frameworks are the most valuable kind.
Adding a vulnerability or threat vector
Check whether it already exists under a different name. Search `data/vulnerabilities.yaml` or `data/threat_vectors.yaml` for related terms before adding a new entry.
Use the next sequential id in the relevant file. Do not renumber or reuse an existing id, since risk registers and reports may reference it.
Write the description in one or two plain-language sentences: what the weakness or attack path is, not why it matters or how severe it is. Severity is assessed per system in the risk register, not fixed in the catalog.
Set `applies_to` honestly. If you are not sure whether an item applies to predictive systems, agentic systems, or both, open the pull request with your best guess and flag the uncertainty in the description; a reviewer with more context can correct it.
For threat vectors, cite the closest maintained framework in the `framework` field. A citation to MITRE ATLAS, the OWASP LLM or Agentic Top 10, or NIST AI 100-2 is strongly preferred. An uncited entry will be merged but flagged for follow-up.
Update the matching narrative doc (`docs/03-vulnerability-catalog.md` or `docs/04-threat-vector-catalog.md`) in the same pull request so the YAML and the prose never drift apart.
Correcting a framework citation
Frameworks referenced here (MITRE ATLAS, OWASP's LLM and Agentic Top 10 lists, NIST AI 100-2) are updated on their own schedules. If a citation is stale, a pull request that updates the `framework` field, along with a link to the current source, is welcome without needing to touch anything else.
Reporting an issue
Use the issue templates in `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/`. Separate templates exist for a new catalog entry and for a correction to an existing one.
